</div>
</div></section></section><section aria-label="Article content"><section class="body main-article-body"><section class="abstract" id="abstract1"><h2>Key Points</h2>
<section id="ab-doi250014-1"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Question</h3>
<p>What are the efficacy and adverse events of ivarmacitinib treatment in adolescents and adults with moderate to severe atopic dermatitis?</p></section><section id="ab-doi250014-2"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Findings</h3>
<p>This phase 3 randomized clinical trial of 336 patients (age ≥12 years) found that significantly more patients in the 4- or 8-mg ivarmacitinib groups achieved an Investigator Global Assessment score of 0 or 1 with at least a 2-grade improvement from baseline and an Eczema Area and Severity Index score improvement of at least 75% compared to the placebo group. Serious treatment-emergent adverse events occurred at low and similar rates across all groups.</p></section><section id="ab-doi250014-3"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Meaning</h3>
<p>These findings show that ivarmacitinib is an effective treatment with a favorable risk-benefit profile for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in adults and adolescents.</p></section></section><section class="abstract" id="abstract2"><h2>Abstract</h2>
<section id="ab-doi250014-4"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Importance</h3>
<p>Ivarmacitinib, a selective oral Janus kinase 1 (JAK1) inhibitor, has demonstrated efficacy for treating adults with moderate to severe atopic dermatitis (AD) in a phase 2 trial.</p></section><section id="ab-doi250014-5"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Objective</h3>
<p>To evaluate the efficacy and adverse events of ivarmacitinib in adolescents and adults with moderate to severe AD.</p></section><section id="ab-doi250014-6"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Design, Setting, and Participants</h3>
<p>This multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase 3 randomized clinical trial included patients aged 12 to 75 years with moderate to severe AD. Patients were enrolled from 53 sites in Canada and China from April 2021 to April 2022. Data were analyzed from July 11 to September 27, 2023.</p></section><section id="ab-doi250014-7"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Interventions</h3>
<p>Patients were randomized (1:1:1) to receive once-daily 4- or 8-mg ivarmacitinib or placebo for 16 weeks.</p></section><section id="ab-doi250014-8"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Main Outcomes and Measures</h3>
<p>Co-primary end points were the proportions of patients achieving an Investigator Global Assessment (IGA) score of 0 (clear) or 1 (almost clear) with at least a 2-grade improvement from baseline and an Eczema Area and Severity Index score improvement of 75% (EASI-75) at week 16.</p></section><section id="ab-doi250014-9"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Results</h3>
<p>Of 336 randomized patients (mean [SD] age, 31.1 [15.4] years; 213 [63.4%] male; 286 [85.1%] Asian), 113 received 4-mg ivarmacitinib, 112 received 8-mg ivarmacitinib, and 111 received placebo. At week 16, significantly more patients in the 4-mg ivarmacitinib group (41 of 113 [36.3%]; 95% CI, 27.5%-45.9%; <em>P</em> &lt; .001) and the 8-mg ivarmacitinib group (47 of 112 [42.0%]; 95% CI, 32.7%-51.7%; <em>P</em> &lt; .001) achieved an IGA score of 0 or 1 with at least a 2-grade improvement compared to the placebo group (10 of 111 [9.0%]; 95% CI, 4.4%-15.9%). EASI-75 responses were also significantly higher in the ivarmacitinib groups: 61 patients (54.0%; 95% CI, 44.4%-63.4%; <em>P</em> &lt; .001) in the 4-mg group, and 74 (66.1%; 95% CI, 56.5%-74.8%; <em>P</em> &lt; .001) in 8-mg group compared to 24 patients (21.6%; 95% CI, 14.4%-30.4%) in the placebo group. Treatment-emergent adverse events were reported by 78 patients (69.0%) in the 4-mg group, 74 (66.1%) in the 8-mg group, and 72 (64.9%) in the placebo group. Serious treatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 3 patients (2.7%) in the 4-mg group, 2 (1.8%) in the 8-mg group, and 3 (2.7%) in the placebo group.</p></section><section id="ab-doi250014-10"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Conclusions and Relevance</h3>
<p>This phase 3 randomized clinical trial determined that once-daily ivarmacitinib demonstrated significant efficacy and a favorable risk-benefit profile for treating moderate to severe AD in adults and adolescents. These results support the potential of ivarmacitinib as a new therapeutic option.</p></section><section id="ab-doi250014-11"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Trial Registration</h3>
<p>ClinicalTrials.gov Identifier: <a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT04875169" class="usa-link" data-ga-action="click_feat_suppl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCT04875169</a></p></section></section><section class="abstract" id="abstract3"><hr class="headless">
<p>This phase 3 randomized clinical trial evaluates the efficacy and adverse effects of ivarmacitinib for treatment of moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in adults and adolescents.</p></section><section id="H1-1-DOI250014"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Introduction</h2>
<p>Atopic dermatitis (AD) is a chronic, relapsing inflammatory skin disorder with immune dysfunction that affects both lesional and nonlesional skin and is characterized by pruritus, skin pain, eczematous lesions, and dry skin.<sup><a href="#doi250014r1" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r1">1</a>,<a href="#doi250014r2" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r2">2</a>,<a href="#doi250014r3" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r3">3</a></sup> AD affects approximately 10% to 20% of children and 2% to 10% of adults,<sup><a href="#doi250014r4" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r4">4</a></sup> often leads to sleep disturbances, and is associated with various comorbidities,<sup><a href="#doi250014r1" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r1">1</a>,<a href="#doi250014r2" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r2">2</a>,<a href="#doi250014r3" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r3">3</a></sup> substantially impairing both physical and psychosocial well-being and reducing quality of life.<sup><a href="#doi250014r5" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r5">5</a>,<a href="#doi250014r6" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r6">6</a></sup></p>
<p>Treatment of moderate to severe AD remains challenging due to the limited efficacy and adverse effects of traditional immunosuppressive agents, which restrict their long-term use.<sup><a href="#doi250014r7" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r7">7</a></sup> Dupilumab, a monoclonal antibody targeting the interleukin-4 (IL-4) receptor α subunit, has been approved for moderate to severe AD.<sup><a href="#doi250014r8" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r8">8</a>,<a href="#doi250014r9" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r9">9</a></sup> However, some patients exhibit inadequate response, and the injectable formulation may be less desirable for others.</p>
<p>The Janus kinase (JAK) family, including JAK1, JAK2, JAK3, and TYK2, regulates the JAK/STAT pathway, which is central to inflammation in AD.<sup><a href="#doi250014r10" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r10">10</a></sup> Key cytokines, such as IL-4, IL-13, IL-31, and thymic stromal lymphopoietin produce AD-related itching and inflammation primarily through JAK1-mediated signaling.<sup><a href="#doi250014r3" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r3">3</a>,<a href="#doi250014r11" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r11">11</a></sup> Chronic pruritus is also influenced by neuronal JAK1 signaling.<sup><a href="#doi250014r12" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r12">12</a></sup> Consequently, targeting JAK1 pathways has become a promising strategy for moderate to severe AD.<sup><a href="#doi250014r13" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r13">13</a>,<a href="#doi250014r14" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r14">14</a></sup> Several oral JAK inhibitors are available for treating AD in adolescents and adults.<sup><a href="#doi250014r15" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r15">15</a></sup> Baricitinib, a JAK1/2 inhibitor, has been approved by the European Medicines Agency for treatment of adult AD.<sup><a href="#doi250014r16" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r16">16</a></sup> Also, upadacitinib and abrocitinib, both selective JAK1 inhibitors, have been approved by the China National Medical Products Administration, the European Medicines Agency, and the US Food and Drug Administration for adolescent and adult AD.<sup><a href="#doi250014r17" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r17">17</a>,<a href="#doi250014r18" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r18">18</a></sup></p>
<p>Ivarmacitinib is a highly selective oral JAK1 inhibitor developed for AD treatment. In vitro studies show it has a 9-fold greater selectivity for JAK1 over JAK2, minimizing JAK2 inhibition and reducing potential risks of neutropenia and anemia.<sup><a href="#doi250014r19" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r19">19</a></sup> A prior phase 2 study (<a href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT04162899" class="usa-link" data-ga-action="click_feat_suppl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCT04162899</a>)<sup><a href="#doi250014r20" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r20">20</a></sup> demonstrated the efficacy and adverse events of ivarmacitinib at 4-mg and 8-mg doses in adults with moderate to severe AD. This phase 3 study was initiated to further evaluate once-daily ivarmacitinib (4- and 8-mg doses) in adults and adolescents with moderate to severe AD.</p></section><section id="H1-2-DOI250014"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Methods</h2>
<p>This study adhered to the Declaration of Helsinki and Good Clinical Practice guidelines. The protocol and amendments were approved by each site’s ethics committee, and patients or their parents/guardians provided written informed consent. This study Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials (<a href="http://www.equator-network.org/reporting-guidelines/consort/" class="usa-link usa-link--external" data-ga-action="click_feat_suppl" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CONSORT</a>) reporting guideline. The trial protocol is available in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 1</a>, and the statistical analysis plan, in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 2</a>.</p>
<section id="H2-1-DOI250014"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Study Design</h3>
<p>This multicenter, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase 3 randomized clinical trial was conducted at 53 hospitals in China and Canada (eTable 1 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>). It included a 5-week screening, a 16-week core treatment period, a 36-week extension (reported elsewhere), and a 4-week follow-up. During the extension, patients in the placebo group switched to ivarmacitinib after completing the core treatment (eFigure 1 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>).</p></section><section id="H2-2-DOI250014"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Study Participants</h3>
<p>Eligible patients were age 12 to 75 years, weighed at least 40 kg, and had a diagnosis of moderate to severe AD per Hanifin and Rajka criteria<sup><a href="#doi250014r21" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r21">21</a></sup> for at least 1 year. Inclusion criteria required at least 10% body surface area (BSA) involvement, an Eczema Area and Severity Index (EASI) score of at least 16, an Investigator Global Assessment (IGA) score of at least 3, and a Worst Itch−Numeric Rating Scale (WI-NRS) score of at least 4. Patients were required to have documented inadequate response or intolerance to topical corticosteroids or calcineurin inhibitors, or a need for systemic therapies within the previous 6 months. Key exclusion criteria included a history of thrombocytopenia, coagulopathy, or any use of systemic JAK inhibitors, glucocorticoids, immunosuppressants, phosphodiesterase inhibitors, or phototherapy within the past 4 weeks; topical treatments for AD within 1 week; or dupilumab within 6 weeks before the first dose of the study treatment. Full eligibility criteria are detailed in the trial protocol (<a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 1</a>).</p></section><section id="H2-3-DOI250014"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Randomization and Masking</h3>
<p>Patients were randomly assigned (1:1:1) to receive once-daily oral ivarmacitinib, 4 or 8 mg, or placebo. Randomization was stratified by baseline disease severity (IGA score, 3 or 4), race (Asian or not Asian), and age (&lt;18 or ≥18 years), using a computer-generated code from an interactive web response system. At week 16, patients in the placebo group, completing the core treatment, were rerandomized (1:1) to ivarmacitinib, 4 or 8 mg, for the extension period, while the other groups continued treatment through week 52. Patients, investigators, and sponsors were blinded to study treatment.</p>
<p>From April 5, 2021, to April 7, 2022, a total of 467 patients were screened, and 336 were randomized to treatment groups. The treatment discontinuation rate was higher in the placebo group (17 of 111 [15.3%]) than in the ivarmacitinib groups (4-mg group: 8 of 113 [7.1%]; 8-mg group: 10 of 112 [8.9%]; <a href="#doi250014f1" class="usa-link">Figure 1</a>).</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="doi250014f1"><h4 class="obj_head">Figure 1.  CONSORT Flow Diagram.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><img class="graphic" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/5e77/12044538/982c8b7c62d8/jamadermatol-e250982-g001.jpg" loading="lazy" height="2350" width="3892" alt="Figure 1. "></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/doi250014f1/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>AD indicates atopic dermatitis, and TEAE, treatment-emergent adverse event.</p></figcaption></figure></section><section id="H2-4-DOI250014"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Outcomes and Measures</h3>
<p>Co-primary end points were the proportion of patients achieving IGA response (clear [0] or almost clear [1] with an improvement of ≥2 grades from baseline) and proportion of patients achieving EASI-75 response (≥75% improvement from baseline), both at week 16. Secondary end points included the proportion of patients with WI-NRS responses (improvement of ≥4 points from baseline) and time to WI-NRS response at scheduled visits (weeks 1, 4, 8, 12, and 16), as well as with EASI-75 and IGA response at weeks 1, 4, 8, and 12.</p>
<p>Additional end points included the proportion of patients achieving: EASI-50 response (improvement of ≥50% from baseline; EASI-90 (improvement of ≥90% from baseline); Scoring for Atopic Dermatitis (SCORAD) improvements (SCORAD50, SCORAD75, and SCORAD90); changes from baseline in EASI, SCORAD, BSA affected, and patient-reported outcomes as measured by the Patient-Oriented Eczema Measure (POEM), Dermatology Life Quality Index (DLQI), and Children’s Dermatology Life Quality Index (CDLQI) tools,<sup><a href="#doi250014r22" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r22">22</a>,<a href="#doi250014r23" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r23">23</a>,<a href="#doi250014r24" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r24">24</a></sup>; and changes from baseline in serum immunoglobulin E (IgE) and peripheral eosinophils at all scheduled visits.</p>
<p>Treatment-emergent adverse events (TEAEs) were continuously monitored by evaluating vital signs, laboratory test results, and electrocardiogram findings, from informed consent through 28 days posttreatment.</p></section><section id="H2-5-DOI250014"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Statistical Analysis</h3>
<p>All randomized patients who received at least 1 dose were included in the full analysis set, and those who received the study treatment were included in the safety analysis set. A total study sample of 330 patients (110 per group) was required to ensure 98% power to detect a 30% improvement in EASI-75 rates and a 25% improvement in IGA response rates for 8-mg ivarmacitinib vs placebo, and 80% power to detect a 20% improvement in EASI-75 and a 15% improvement in IGA response rates for 4-mg ivarmacitinib vs placebo. The placebo response rates were assumed to be 15% for EASI-75 and 10% for IGA at week 16.</p>
<p>For the primary end point, a hierarchical testing procedure controlled the 2-sided type I error at .05, starting with 8-mg ivarmacitinib followed by 4-mg if superiority was confirmed. Secondary and additional end points were tested at a nominal 5% significance level without multiplicity control.</p>
<p>Co-primary end points were analyzed using Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel tests, adjusted for randomization strata. Nonresponder imputation (NRI) was used for missing week 16 data, discontinuations due to lack of efficacy, death, or use of prohibited AD medications before week 16. Response rate differences between ivarmacitinib groups and placebo, with 95% CIs estimated by the Miettinen-Nurminen method, were reported.</p>
<p>Other binary end points were analyzed similarly using NRI for missing values. Continuous end points were assessed using a mixed-effects model for repeated measures, incorporating treatment group, randomization strata, visit, treatment-by-visit interaction, and baseline values as covariates. Treatment effects were calculated at week 16 and other time points (weeks 1, 4, 8, 12). Least squares (LS) mean changes from baseline with 95% CI were reported for each group, along with LS mean differences between ivarmacitinib groups and placebo. The median time to first WI-NRS response and 95% CIs were estimated using the Kaplan-Meier method and were compared via a log-rank test. Safety data were summarized descriptively. Data analyses were performed from July 11 to September 27, 2023, using SAS, version 9.4 (SAS Institute). The Statistical Analysis Plan provides additional details (<a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 2</a>).</p></section></section><section id="H1-3-DOI250014"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Results</h2>
<p>The analysis included 336 patients (mean [SD] age, 31.1 [15.4] years; 213 [63.4%] male and 123 [36.6%] female; 286 Asian [85.1%] and 35 White [10.4%] individuals). Demographic and baseline characteristics were balanced across treatment groups (<a href="#doi250014t1" class="usa-link">Table 1</a>). Overall, 195 patients (58.0%) had moderate disease, and 141 (42.0%) had severe disease as assessed by IGA score; 132 (39.3%) had received systemic treatments; and 318 (94.6%) had used topical corticosteroids before the study.</p>
