<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="table/doi250014t2/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<div class="tw-foot p">
<div class="fn" id="_fn_p54"><p>Abbreviations: CDLQI, Children’s Dermatology Life Quality Index; DLQI, Dermatology Life Quality Index; EASI, Eczema Area and Severity Index; IGA, Investigator Global Assessment; LS, least squares; NA, not applicable; POEM, Patient-Oriented Eczema Measure; SCORAD50/75/90, Scoring of Atopic Dermatitis Index (change of ≥50%, ≥75%, or ≥90%); WI-NRS, Worst Itch−Numeric Rating scale.</p></div>
<div class="fn" id="doi250014t2n1">
<sup>
<sup>a</sup>
</sup><p class="display-inline">Corresponding responses at week 16 analyzed using nonresponder imputation (NRI).</p>
</div>
<div class="fn" id="doi250014t2n2">
<sup>
<sup>b</sup>
</sup><p class="display-inline">95% CIs were calculated using the normal approximation method.</p>
</div>
<div class="fn" id="doi250014t2n3">
<sup>
<sup>c</sup>
</sup><p class="display-inline">Proportion difference and 95% CI were calculated using the Miettinen-Nurminen method.</p>
</div>
<div class="fn" id="doi250014t2n4">
<sup>
<sup>d</sup>
</sup><p class="display-inline">Binary end points comparisons at week 16 between the 4- and 8-mg groups vs placebo calculated using Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel tests, adjusted for randomization strata. For the co-primary end points, NRI was applied to patients with missing values at week 16, for discontinued treatment due to lack of efficacy or death, and for protocol-prohibited AD medications prior to week 16. For other binary end points at week 16, NRI was implemented for patients with missing values at scheduled visits.</p>
</div>
<div class="fn" id="doi250014t2n5">
<sup>
<sup>e</sup>
</sup><p class="display-inline">Number of patients with nonmissing values</p>
</div>
<div class="fn" id="doi250014t2n6">
<sup>
<sup>f</sup>
</sup><p class="display-inline">Comparisons between the 4- and 8-mg groups vs placebo were conducted using a mixed-effects model for repeated measures (MMRM). This model incorporated fixed effects for the treatment group, randomization strata, visit, treatment-by-visit interaction, and the respective baseline value. Within the MMRM framework, treatment effect differences were assessed not only at the prespecified primary time point (week 16) but also at other time points (weeks 1, 4, 8, and 12) through time point-specific contrasts derived from the MMRM model.</p>
</div>
</div></section><p>The proportion of patients achieving IGA or EASI-75 responses was higher in both of the ivarmacitinib groups at each scheduled visit, with improvements from baseline to week 16 (<a href="#doi250014f2" class="usa-link">Figure 2</a>). At week 16, placebo-adjusted LS mean changes in EASI scores were –6.3 (95% CI, –9.0 to –3.6; <em>P</em> &lt; .001) for the 4-mg group and –6.8 (95% CI, –9.5 to –4.0; <em>P</em> &lt; .001) for the 8-mg group (eTable 2 and eFigure 4 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>). Proportions of patients achieving EASI-50 and EASI-90 were also higher in the ivarmacitinib groups compared to placebo, with improvements from baseline to week 16 (eFigure 5 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>).</p>
<p>During the 16-week period, a higher proportion of patients in the 4- and 8-mg ivarmacitinib groups achieved at least a 4-point improvement in WI-NRS compared to placebo (42 of 113 [37.2%] in the 4-mg group and 45 of 112 [40.2%] in the 8-mg group vs 14 of 111 [12.6%] in the placebo group). Notably, improvements were observed as early as day 3 in the 8-mg group, and day 8 in the 4-mg group. The median time to the first WI-NRS response was 23 days (95% CI, 10.0-30.0) for the 8-mg group, 36 days (95% CI, 22.0-51.0) for the 4-mg group, and 144 days (95% CI, not evaluable) for the placebo group (eFigure 6 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>).</p>
<p>At week 16, the placebo-adjusted LS mean difference in SCORAD total scores was −12.8 (95% CI, −17.7 to −7.9) for the 4-mg group, and −16.5 (95% CI, −21.8 to −11.2) for the 8-mg group (all <em>P</em> &lt; .001; eTable 2 and eFigure 7 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>). Both ivarmacitinib groups showed improvements in SCORAD50, SCORAD75, and SCORAD90, from baseline to week 16 (<a href="#doi250014t2" class="usa-link">Table 2</a>; eFigure 8 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>).</p>
<p>At week 16, the placebo-adjusted LS mean changes in BSA affected were –11.6% (95% CI, –16.4% to –6.7%; <em>P</em> &lt; .001) for the 4-mg group, and –13.9% (95% CI, –18.6% to –9.2%; <em>P</em> &lt; .001) for the 8-mg group (eTable 2 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>). Both ivarmacitinib groups showed improvements in BSA from week 1 to week 16 compared to placebo (eFigure 9 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>).</p>
<p>At week 16, placebo-adjusted LS mean changes in DLQI/CDLQI scores were –4.4 (95% CI, –6.1 to –2.7; <em>P</em> &lt; .001) for the 4-mg group, and –5.2 (95% CI, –7.1 to –3.3; <em>P</em> &lt; .001) for the 8-mg group (<a href="#doi250014t2" class="usa-link">Table 2</a>). Similarly, placebo-adjusted LS mean changes in POEM scores were –5.2 (95% CI, –7.3 to –3.2; <em>P</em> &lt; .001) for the 4-mg group, and –7.7 (95% CI, –9.8 to –5.5; <em>P</em> &lt; .001) for the 8-mg group. Notable reductions in DLQI/CDLQI and POEM scores were observed from week 4 to week 16 in both of the ivarmacitinib groups (eFigure 10 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>). Serum IgE levels and peripheral blood eosinophil counts did not show significant changes from baseline in either ivarmacitinib group compared to placebo (eTable 2 and eFigure 11 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>).</p></section><section id="H2-7-DOI250014"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Adverse Events</h3>
<p>Overall, 78 patients in the 4-mg ivarmacitinib group (69.0%), 74 in the 8-mg ivarmacitinib group (66.1%), and 72 in the placebo group (64.9%) reported TEAEs of associated with any cause during the core treatment period (<a href="#doi250014t3" class="usa-link">Table 3</a>). The most frequently reported TEAEs with ivarmacitinib were upper respiratory tract infection (12 of 113 [10.6%] in the 4-mg group, 13 of 112 [11.6%] in the 8-mg group vs 12 of 111 [10.8%] in the placebo group) and increased blood creatine phosphokinase (8 of 113 [7.1%] in the 4-mg group, 12 of 112 [10.7%] in the 8-mg group vs 1 of 111 [0.9%] in the placebo group).</p>
