<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="table/doi250014t3/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<div class="tw-foot p">
<div class="fn" id="_fn_p67"><p>Abbreviations: AESI, adverse events of special interest; MACE, major adverse cardiac events; TEAE, treatment-emergent adverse event.</p></div>
<div class="fn" id="doi250014t3n1">
<sup>
<sup>a</sup>
</sup><p class="display-inline">Serious infections included tuberculosis, herpes zoster, opportunistic infections, and any bacterial, viral, or fungal infections requiring hospitalization and intravenous antimicrobial treatment for at least 3 days.</p>
</div>
<div class="fn" id="doi250014t3n2">
<sup>
<sup>b</sup>
</sup><p class="display-inline">Included pulmonary embolism, venous thrombosis (eg, deep vein thrombosis), and arterial embolism.</p>
</div>
<div class="fn" id="doi250014t3n3">
<sup>
<sup>c</sup>
</sup><p class="display-inline">Included cardiovascular death, nonfatal myocardial infarction, and nonfatal stroke (including reversible focal neurological deficits supported by imaging evidence of ischemic or hemorrhagic brain injury).</p>
</div>
</div></section><p>Serious TEAEs were reported in 3 patients (2.7%) in the 4-mg group, 2 (1.8%) in the 8-mg group, and 3 (2.7%) in the placebo group (<a href="#doi250014t3" class="usa-link">Table 3</a>). Most serious TEAEs improved or resolved and were deemed not related to the study treatment. Serious TEAEs that were considered to be related to treatment were reported in 2 patients (1.8%) in the 4-mg group; specifically, 1 patient developed sepsis and the other developed varicella (ivarmacitinib treatment was discontinued and both patients recovered) (eTable 3 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>).</p>
<p>Overall, 3 patients (2.7%) in the 4-mg group, 4 (3.6%) in the 8-mg group, and 6 (5.4%) in the placebo group discontinued treatment due to TEAEs. The most frequently reported TEAE leading to treatment discontinuation was worsening of AD, which occurred in 1 patient (0.9%) in the 4-mg group and 2 (1.8%) in the placebo group (eTable 4 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>).</p>
<p>Protocol-defined adverse events of special interest (AESI) occurred in 2 patients (1.8%) in the 4-mg group and 1 patient (0.9%) in the placebo group, with none reported in the 8-mg group. All of these AESIs were serious infections: 1 case of varicella and 1 of sepsis in the 4-mg group, and 1 case of COVID-19 pneumonia in the placebo group (<a href="#doi250014t3" class="usa-link">Table 3</a>). No cases of thrombosis, malignant neoplasms, major adverse cardiac events (MACE), or deaths were reported.</p>
<p>Laboratory assessments showed that the ivarmacitinib groups had higher levels of creatine phosphokinase, low-density lipoprotein cholesterol, and triglycerides, as well as decreases in platelet and neutrophil counts compared to placebo (eTables 5 and 6 in <a href="#note-DOI250014-1-s" class="usa-link">Supplement 3</a>). These changes were transient, self-limiting, and clinically insignificant. No significant trends were observed in other hematological or biochemical parameters, and changes in electrocardiogram measurements and vital signs were not clinically relevant.</p></section></section><section id="H1-4-DOI250014"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Discussion</h2>
<p>This phase 3 study met its co-primary efficacy end points, demonstrating that ivarmacitinib, 4 and 8 mg, once daily were effective for adolescents and adults with moderate to severe AD. Similar efficacy trends were observed in phase 3 trials of other JAK1 inhibitors, such as abrocitinib (JADE MONO-1 and JADE MONO-2)<sup><a href="#doi250014r18" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r18">18</a>,<a href="#doi250014r25" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r25">25</a></sup> and upadacitinib (Measure Up 1 and Measure Up 2),<sup><a href="#doi250014r17" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r17">17</a></sup> with higher proportions of patients achieving IGA and EASI-75 responses compared to placebo.</p>
<p>A dose-dependent efficacy was observed with ivarmacitinib, with the 8-mg dose showing higher response rates for IGA (42.0% vs 36.3%) and EASI-75 (66.1% vs 54.0%) compared to the 4-mg dose at week 16. Although no statistical comparisons were made between doses, the 8-mg dose indicated numerically greater efficacy across all end points while maintaining a manageable adverse event profile, consistent with phase 2 findings.<sup><a href="#doi250014r20" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r20">20</a></sup> Ivarmacitinib at both doses showed clinically meaningful IGA and EASI-75 responses as early as week 4, with continued improvement through week 16, supporting phase 2 findings.<sup><a href="#doi250014r20" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r20">20</a></sup> Subgroup analyses revealed consistent efficacy in adolescents (17.3%), underscoring ivarmacitinib’s effectiveness in this patient population.</p>
<p>The higher response rates with both ivarmacitinib doses, measured by EASI-90 and SCORAD75 or 90 at week 16, are clinically meaningful. These metrics reflect the impact of AD on physical symptoms and overall well-being, serving as key benchmarks for evaluating therapies and setting treatment goals.<sup><a href="#doi250014r26" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r26">26</a>,<a href="#doi250014r27" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r27">27</a></sup></p>
<p>Pruritus, a key symptom of AD that affects quality of life,<sup><a href="#doi250014r7" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r7">7</a>,<a href="#doi250014r28" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r28">28</a></sup> was significantly improved in both ivarmacitinib groups. Clinically meaningful reduction in pruritus (WI-NRS response) was observed as early as day 3 with the 8-mg dose, and day 8 with the 4-mg dose, with sustained relief through week 16. This rapid, sustained response, similar to that experienced with other JAK1 inhibitors,<sup><a href="#doi250014r17" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r17">17</a>,<a href="#doi250014r18" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r18">18</a>,<a href="#doi250014r25" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r25">25</a></sup> may explain the substantial improvements in quality of life observed in this study. The mechanism likely involves inhibiting pruritogenic cytokines and direct action on neuronal JAK1 signaling.<sup><a href="#doi250014r29" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r29">29</a>,<a href="#doi250014r30" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r30">30</a></sup></p>
<p>The adverse event profile of ivarmacitinib was consistent with the findings of prior studies, with no new risks identified.<sup><a href="#doi250014r17" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r17">17</a>,<a href="#doi250014r18" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r18">18</a>,<a href="#doi250014r25" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r25">25</a>,<a href="#doi250014r31" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r31">31</a>,<a href="#doi250014r32" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r32">32</a>,<a href="#doi250014r33" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r33">33</a></sup> The most common TEAEs included upper respiratory infection, increased blood creatine phosphokinase, proteinuria, hyperlipidemia, folliculitis, and acne. No hematological abnormalities were observed, and hemoglobin, neutrophil, and lymphocyte counts remained stable. The incidence of serious TEAEs was low (&lt;3%) in both of the ivarmacitinib groups and comparable to placebo, with most events not considered to be treatment-related. Discontinuations due to TEAEs were lower in the ivarmacitinib groups (2.7% and 3.6%) compared to placebo (5.4%). Serious infections, MACE, thrombosis, and malignant neoplasms are known potential risks associated with JAK inhibitors.<sup><a href="#doi250014r15" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r15">15</a></sup> In this study, 3 patients experienced serious infections: 1 with varicella and another with sepsis in the ivarmacitinib 4-mg group, and 1 with COVID-19 pneumonia in the placebo group. No MACE, thrombosis, or malignant neoplasms were reported. Long-term data are needed to assess the risks associated with these concerning TEAEs. Interestingly, the incidence of acne in patients receiving ivarmacitinib (4-mg group, 3.5%; 8-mg group, 6.3%) was comparable to that reported for abrocitinib (&lt;5.0%)<sup><a href="#doi250014r18" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r18">18</a></sup> and lower than that reported for upadacitinib (8.5%-12.3%).<sup><a href="#doi250014r17" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r17">17</a></sup></p>
<section id="H2-8-DOI250014"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Limitations</h3>
<p>This study had several limitations. First, the placebo-controlled period was limited to 16 weeks, and long-term data are needed to fully assess the efficacy and adverse events in patients with AD; these should be reported separately. Second, the predominance of Asian patients may limit the generalizability of the findings to other populations, although AD is more prevalent and severe in Asian patients compared to White patients.<sup><a href="#doi250014r34" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r34">34</a>,<a href="#doi250014r35" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="doi250014r35">35</a></sup></p></section></section><section id="H1-5-DOI250014"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Conclusions</h2>
<p>This phase 3 randomized clinical trial found that once-daily oral ivarmacitinib (4 or 8 mg) significantly improved the signs and symptoms of moderate to severe AD in adolescent and adult patients compared to placebo, demonstrating a favorable benefit-risk profile. Ivarmacitinib represents a promising new treatment option for patients whose AD is not adequately controlled by topical therapies.</p></section><section id="note-DOI250014-1"><section class="sm xbox font-sm" id="note-DOI250014-1-s"><div class="caption p">
